A||N||43||Ralph||Clutch Fix Revealed||||1||
Z||000000||Ralph||12-31-2000||03:05 PM||||For a couple of years I have read everything there is about the clutch problem on the 4 Cyl 5 Speed cars. The same problem does not appear to exist on V6 cars. I have read every conceiveable message there is on several Forums and discussion groups. I believe I have tried any and everything there has been suggested by everyone including the experts. Every single method of bleeding has been tried by me. Brand new Master and Slave from the dealer were also tried as well as new after market (Wagner) Parts without any reasonable degree of Success. I even tried the ATF method which I admit did work well till it destroyed the rubber parts. The problem with the clutch just barely releasing or just barely NOT releasing is what I have had all along. This is the same problem that many others have had. The master and the slave are the same diameter and X amount of push at the pedal produces the exact same amount of push at the slave cylinder which is barely enough travel with all new parts. My fix addresses this problem and effectively reduces the amount of travel required by the slave. Some of you are going to say it is a bad way to do it. Some will agree with me. The main thing is, IT WORKS !. What I did was to fabricate a clutch lever return stop. This stop limits the amount travel in the release direction. The stop is fully adjustable an you can adjust the spot where the clutch engages off of the floor. After several experimental adjustments, I settled on stopping the lever 3/32 to 1/8 inch of travel before it reached the relaxed position. This effectively puts unwanted throwout bearing contact with the clutch disk, but it is such a slight amount I believe it will only shorten the life of the throwout bearing a miniscule amount. I believe I could handle dropping the cradle once every 4 or 5 years to replace a throwout bearing a lot easier than bleeding that dog-gone system twice a week or more sometimes, not to mention the wear and tear on the ttransmission and the shifting mechanisms... Mine has been usiung this fix now for more than two months. What a relief it is to know that I can hop into my car and know the clutch will release and engage perfectly every time.<P>You can see a photo (with callouts) at:<BR> <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=95670&a=698688&p=36673827&Sequence=0&res=high" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=95670&a=698688&p=36673827&Sequence=0&res=high</A> <P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/yellowformula/LittleFiero.jpg"> <BR>  <B>  Ralph </B>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||12-31-2000||03:52 PM||||Two hours average driving with a correctly adjusted clutch linkage will give the throwout bearing about one to three minutes of spin time.  The same drive with the linkage adjusted to barely touch the throwout bearing results in one hundred twenty minutes of spin time.<P>Does it matter?  I have NEVER worn out a throwout bearing, even in fifth-hand beaters with high milage.  Luck or else the bearings are really overbuilt.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Phil||12-31-2000||06:31 PM||||OR-- one could just fabricate a slightly shorter clutch release arm.||||reg||1
Z||000003||polomeno||12-31-2000||06:47 PM||||how does stoping the release lever from moving affect how far the slave cylinder must move???  either way the salve is still going to have to move the same amount to push the lever??  it's just not going to be pushing it as soon? i am having alot of clutch problems and will definetly try  it if it works <P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||12-31-2000||08:48 PM||||Just mulling your "fix" over in my mind. All you are doing is slightly depressing your clutch all the time- in normal operation the only thing that moves the clutch arm back is the pressure of the pressure plate diaphgram/fingers pushing on the TO bearing so the TO bearing is always resting against the pressure plate. The only ways to increase throw is to either shorten the clutch arm or make sure you are getting full throw in the MC. To be sure of max throw in the MC check for "slop" in the pedal linkage or make the pedal to MC rod a touch longer(makes the pedal higher than the brake). This all assumes that the whole system has neither leaks or air||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||01-01-2001||02:41 AM||||Theoretically, it is a good idea. With the stop, it SHOULD add a few mm of travel,  AS LONG as you bleed it after you add the stop (other wise, you would lose travel).||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ralph||01-01-2001||07:08 AM||||I agree... In my lifetime of drivig and owning cars, I never had a throwout bearing go.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phaeton:<BR><B>Two hours average driving with a correctly adjusted clutch linkage will give the throwout bearing about one to three minutes of spin time.  The same drive with the linkage adjusted to barely touch the throwout bearing results in one hundred twenty minutes of spin time.<P>Does it matter?  I have NEVER worn out a throwout bearing, even in fifth-hand beaters with high milage.  Luck or else the bearings are really overbuilt.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/yellowformula/LittleFiero.jpg"> <BR>  <B>  Ralph </B>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ralph||01-01-2001||07:10 AM||||Won't work...Wont be the same... Clutch would only re-adjust itself to the previous problem. I am assuming you mean shorten the push rod.  If you were to actually shorten the release arm (which would be a major project) it might work since it would effectively re-locate the fulcrum (pivot point) However this method would not have any thing to fall back on. With my method the adjustment of the length of the all thread is all that is necessary and it only takes a few seconds to do that.<P><BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR><B>OR-- one could just fabricate a slightly shorter clutch release arm.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/yellowformula/LittleFiero.jpg">  <BR>  <B>  Ralph </B><p>[This message has been edited by Ralph (edited 01-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ralph||01-01-2001||07:23 AM||||Good question Mr. Polomeno...<BR>When thr release lever returns it pushes fluid back into the master cylinder the exact same amount as before. Lets say for explanation that the master has the ability to push 1 inch of fluid into the slave and when the lever returns it pushes back the same 1 inch of fluid. With the stop I fabricated the release lever is already pushed 1/8 inch, so now when the pedal is pushed it will still move its fluid 1 inch and the slave will also push 1 inch and the lever will move 1 inch, but the lever already had a 1/8 inch head start on the whole works so you effectively depress the clutch lever 1-1/8 inch.<BR>I hope this helps explain....<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by polomeno:<BR><B>how does stoping the release lever from moving affect how far the slave cylinder must move???  either way the salve is still going to have to move the same amount to push the lever??  it's just not going to be pushing it as soon? i am having alot of clutch problems and will definetly try  it if it works <P>Thanks</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/yellowformula/LittleFiero.jpg"> <BR>  <B>  Ralph </B>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ralph||01-01-2001||07:37 AM||||Try this phil....Take a piece of wood dowell, a tape measure and a saw. measure a spot where you can put a piece of dowell between the slave cylinder bracket and the release lever, now cut the dowell 1/4 inch(just to be sure it's enough)longer than the space. Pry the lever so you can insert the dowell. Be sure the clutch rod stays in place. Now gently with your fingers press in the pedal till resistance is felt (when the push rod is re-seated) Now release your fingers. The master has just gotten a second gulp of fluid and ready. Get someone to hold the dowell in place or do som magic with duct tape so it will stay in place. Now jump in your car, start it up and just move it in the driveway. You will know immediately if it works. Your engagement spot on the pedal will be higher off of the floor than it was. You will also note that it is not necessary to do any bleeding.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR><B>Just mulling your "fix" over in my mind. All you are doing is slightly depressing your clutch all the time- in normal operation the only thing that moves the clutch arm back is the pressure of the pressure plate diaphgram/fingers pushing on the TO bearing so the TO bearing is always resting against the pressure plate. The only ways to increase throw is to either shorten the clutch arm or make sure you are getting full throw in the MC. To be sure of max throw in the MC check for "slop" in the pedal linkage or make the pedal to MC rod a touch longer(makes the pedal higher than the brake). This all assumes that the whole system has neither leaks or air</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/yellowformula/LittleFiero.jpg"> <BR>  <B>  Ralph </B>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ralph||01-01-2001||07:42 AM||||Johnny, See my reply above.  Bleedind is not necessary. The system automaticall re-adjusts it self. (It probly thinks the clutch disk is worn a bit and must compensate) <P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>Theoretically, it is a good idea. With the stop, it SHOULD add a few mm of travel,  AS LONG as you bleed it after you add the stop (other wise, you would lose travel).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/yellowformula/LittleFiero.jpg"> <BR>  <B>  Ralph </B>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phil||01-01-2001||08:04 AM||||I know exactly what you're doing- you are partialy depressing your clutch mechanicly then fully depressing it with the hydraulics. But using your system you are constantly "riding" the clutch but it seems not enough the hamper operation. Hey whatever works.||||reg||1
Z||000012||polomeno||01-01-2001||01:02 PM||||Thanks for the response and good idea Ralph.. my clutch is a piece of ****  and if this workes to make it not such a pain in the ass while i wait to repace the clutch ( it's way too damn cold to do it now ) then i'll try it.. i have a 84 4 cylinder and i'm gonna try  your method this week if i have time<P>thanks <P>John||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ralph||01-02-2001||08:03 AM||||I appreciate the confidence in my fix John. I am sure you will be amazed at the difference. I am also sure that anyone else that has that "unfixable" clutch problem will also be pleased with the results.<BR> <BR>You must remove the rearmost mounting stud from the flange on the slave cylinder and drill out the hole or re-thread the hole with a 5/16-18 tap. I chose the re-thread method cause it could be done on the car without any fluid loss. This requires only one locking nut (shown in the photo). If you drill for the allthread to pass through the flange it will require two lock nuts,  The two nuts on the other end of the allthread that act as the lever stop are a bit larger than the usual 5/16 nut. I found them in my junk box.  <BR> <BR>Please advise the discussion group how things go.<BR> <BR>Ralph<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by polomeno:<BR><B>Thanks for the response and good idea Ralph.. my clutch is a piece of ****  and if this workes to make it not such a pain in the ass while i wait to repace the clutch ( it's way too damn cold to do it now ) then i'll try it.. i have a 84 4 cylinder and i'm gonna try  your method this week if i have time<P>thanks <P>John</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/yellowformula/LittleFiero.jpg"> <BR>  <B>  Ralph </B>||||reg||1
Z||000014||polomeno||01-02-2001||10:54 AM||||Ok.. i'm going to try to make it out to the hardware store today to pick up what i need.. my only concern is that i don't believe i have that same slave mount on my 84 4speed that you have on yours.. i'll have to check, if not i'll try to improvise with something else and i'll take pics if it all works.<P>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000015||Fiero Fanatic||01-03-2001||11:52 AM||||I'm not sure what you did. But this is how I fixed mine and have never had any shifting or bleeding problems since. Full instructions or posted under: Slave Cylinder. Or to see a diagram click on: <A HREF="http://www.eccentrix.com/business/cityselect/diagram.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eccentrix.com/business/cityselect/diagram.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Fiero Fanatic (edited 01-14-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000016||Fiero Fanatic||01-03-2001||11:55 AM||||I think what you did is the equivelent of just installing a longer rod in your slave cylinder? In my 84 it appeared to me that that problem lies with the construction of the clutch pedal allowing air in the clutch master cylinder. The plunger in the C.M.C. allows air in the system. Trust me I've had the best clutch shop in my hometown not even figure this one out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<p>[This message has been edited by Fiero Fanatic (edited 01-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||DJRice||01-03-2001||10:50 PM||||My dad took the business end of a medium sized phillips screwdriver and fabbed a longer slave cylinder push rod and Im pretty sure it accomplished the same thing as what your proposing. <P>Like Phil said...Whatever works!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000018||Fiero Fanatic||01-04-2001||12:32 PM||||Before my wonderful clutch invention I did try installing a longer push-rod in my slave cylinder. All I did was go down to my local clutch shop and ask them if they have and longer push-rods around. They had a coffee can full of them. I grabbed a couple of them and pre-fabed a few different lengths. But I found that by doing this it causes the thowout bearing to ride the pressure plate. Seriously though it's the metal construction of the clutch pedal. If you take off the circlip in the clutch master cylinder you'll see that the plunger allows air into the system. Thus causing constant bad shifting. You can bleed it a 1000 times and it will not get any better. What people need is better clutch pedal construction!||||reg||1
Z||000019||Ralph||01-04-2001||05:44 PM||||I think your idea deserves a lot of merrit. I did a few times get the clutch to release just off of the floor a few times after careful and deliberate bleeding but to only last several days. I always had the feeling that air was getting into the system. The time I tried ATF convinced me that the brake fluid was so thin it couldn't seal the o rings and other parts positively. I went back to the brake fluid after the ATF and my original problem re-appeared magically. It must be the lock washer you have added locates the plunger just enough forward to clear the defect that is in the rearmost part of the cylinder wall of the master. Am I anywhere correct in this understanding ?<P>The only hole in your fix that I can find to poke at is, since I installed my fix, I have had a perfectly operating pedal for about a month now. I did nothing to the master or to the slave cylinder it self to end any induction of air into the system, yet my pedal stills stays comfortably adjusted.  <P>A longer push rod is the same end result as my fix. It pushes the slave piston against the back and uses that to push the clutch lever against the throwout bearing. It's main problems is the hit and miss method to determine the needed length and once found there is no adjustment wihthout modifying parts or making new ones. My fix uses only the bare minimum adjustment to make it dependable without undue stress on the throwout bearing. The adjustment can be changed at will in a matter of about a  minute. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero Fanatic:<BR><B>Before my wonderful clutch invention I did try installing a longer push-rod in my slave cylinder. All I did was go down to my local clutch shop and ask them if they have and longer push-rods around. They had a coffee can full of them. I grabbed a couple of them and pre-fabed a few different lengths. But I found that by doing this it causes the thowout bearing to ride the pressure plate. Seriously though it's the metal construction of the clutch pedal. If you take off the circlip in the clutch master cylinder you'll see that the plunger allows air into the system. Thus causing constant bad shifting. You can bleed it a 1000 times and it will not get any better. What people need is better clutch pedal construction!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/yellowformula/LittleFiero.jpg"> <BR>  <B>  Ralph </B>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Fiero Fanatic||01-05-2001||09:51 PM||||Like I said I did install a longer push-rod in the slave cylinder. It kept constant pressure on the bearing. Over time I kept having to install longer rods. Finally my bearing chewed right through the pressure plate. This is the second time this happened. The first time this automotive shop drilled new holes and mounted the slave cylinder closer to the release lever. It also chewed a hole right through the pressure plate. So, 2 times did I go to the shop and have clutch work done to the total of over $1000.00 !! I got tired of paying this. It seemed like it was becoming an anual thing. Since What I did it's been almost 2 years now, and never again have I ever had any shifting problems or ever had to bleed the system again. Right now I get to fix my timing gear...Oh Joy! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000021||Fiero Fanatic||01-07-2001||01:57 PM||||How can you be sure about that undo stress on your bearing? I'm wondering?||||reg||1
Z||000022||Ralph||01-07-2001||06:19 PM||||I am placing some slight stress on the throwout bearing and I can say slight because I am only pressing the lever a very small amount. If I understand the workings of the self adjusting feature, the fluid presses the lever slightly against the throwout bearing all of the time. That is why there is never any adjustment needed. It's kinda like a person who constantly has their left foot on the clutch pedal and never really takes his foot off. "Riding" This also is not good for the bearing, but doesn't shorten it's life any appreciable amount.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero Fanatic:<BR><B>How can you be sure about that undo stress on your bearing? I'm wondering?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/yellowformula/LittleFiero.jpg"> <BR>  <B>  Ralph </B>||||reg||1
Z||000023||Fiero Fanatic||01-08-2001||05:29 PM||||Unnecessary pressure will cause the throwout bearing grease to burn up. And Burn it will. Burn baby burn. Listen for the bearing to squeal like a pig before it finally blows to bits. Make sure you clean all the pieces out of your pressure plate when you replace the next bearing. Also check the condition of the clutch fork.<P>I learned from this experience. There's no good shortcuts for lack of GM craftmanship. <p>[This message has been edited by Fiero Fanatic (edited 01-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||FIEROGOD||01-10-2001||08:23 PM||||Nobody said anything about the clutch pedal.<BR>I have replaced a few. The problem is the master cylinder rod bottoms out before the pedal hits the floor. I have noticed this problem on Fieros with slave problems. If the clutch doesn't disengage a person has a tendency to push on the pedal harder. This bends the clutch pedal. It may not look bent but if the hydrolics are not leaking that is most likely what it is. Extending the clutch slave rod places extra stress on the throw-out bearing and may make the clutch slip.||||reg||1
Z||000025||MrPBody||01-10-2001||08:49 PM||||Maybe I'm mentioning something you already know about . . .<P>If your car originally had an aluminum clutch pedal which has been replaced by a steel pedal, the master cylinder pushrod may fit loosely on the new pedal.  Rodney sells a replacement bushing to take-up the slack.<P>My car had the reverse problem:  the clutch pedal would not retract far enough, so the master cylinder never fully returned, causing the hydraulics to pump-up in traffic.  I bent the clutch pedal stop back to allow slack in the mechanism.<P>Diff'rent strokes.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000026||Fiero Fanatic||01-10-2001||09:23 PM||||Nobody will ever know what I'm talking about until they look for themselves. You have to take off the circlip in the clutch master cylinder and examine the distance between the push-rod and the plunger.||||reg||1
Z||000027||vtail||01-12-2001||03:30 PM||||I am facing this problem on a 85 V6 SE I got. Car was setting, I replaced the broken slave cylinder bracket and cannot get a clean clutch release. Would it be possible to find a different master cylinder with a bigger bore that would move more fliud to the slave cylinder, hence more travel? I would like to solve this as my 85 4 cly is automatic and not as fun.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Fiero Fanatic||01-12-2001||04:44 PM||||I never heard of a clutch master cylinder with a bigger bore for the Fiero. Before I created my permanent clutch fix. I had replaced my clutch master and slave cylinder. It was a waste of money, because I didn't need them after all. But GM should fabricate a better clutch master cylinder push-rod. Everyone is skeptical about having problems with their clutch pedal assembly, because everyone assumes it's with the hydraulics. The plunger in the clutch master cylinder does extend past the end of the bore. Therefor allowing in  air into the system. But, it takes getting under the dash to examine the clutch pedal alignment and the push-rod to plunger unit. Most Fiero owners just seem to not want to do that. They'd rather do the quick fix and put extra strain on the clutch or throwout bearing.<p>[This message has been edited by Fiero Fanatic (edited 01-12-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000029||Phil||01-12-2001||05:50 PM||||Just to add some fuel to the fire- I'm running a McCleod clutch and I've heard that guys with Center force have the same problem. TOO MUCH THROW. I had to put a pedal stop in mine to cut the throw down.||||reg||1
Z||000030||vtail||01-12-2001||09:03 PM||||I tend to agree with Fiero Fanatics conclusions as far as the clutch/master cylinder set up. However I cannot get to the picture of your solution as the link shows the page is not available. I know that I have play between the pedal push rod and the master cylinder plunger. I was thinking about cutting it and putting in a turn buckle so I could adjust out that play. Would that help? ||||reg||1
Z||000031||bubbajoexx||01-12-2001||11:25 PM||||I have found that if you use the master from a z24 the clutch proble is solved but you must know how to attach the steel line to the plastic master  this master will give you all kinds of push to release the clutch||||reg||1
Z||000032||Fiero Fanatic||01-13-2001||05:43 PM||||I'll fix that link. I thought it was working...OK it's fixed, and here's the link once again <A HREF="http://www.eccentrix.com/business/cityselect/diagram.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.eccentrix.com/business/cityselect/diagram.html</A> <BR>If you  only remove your circlip on your clutch master cylinder. And leave the push-rod attached to the clutch pedal. Line up the push-rod to the end of the plunger. You'll notice that the plunger extends past the end of the bore on the clutch master cylinder. So it's obviously allowing air into the system. Case closed. Now to correct the problem. To keep the plunger from traveling that far back, you need to install a lock-washer (found that it's easier to install) between the retaining washer and the round-end of the push-rod. You must first disconnect the push-rod from the clutch pedal to perform this procedure (It's easier). Install the lock washer (About the size of a dime). Then re-install the push-rod back into the bore of the clutch master cylinder. Re-attach the circlip. Install the push-rod back on to the clutch pedal. Bleed system if necessary. Here's what I'm wondering? If anyone has the red Haynes manual for the Fiero, turn to page 190. Look at diagram 4.5 and take notice of the push-rod and ask yourself "What is that circular thing between the #12 retaining washer and the round end of the push-rod. The book doesn't explain. But I have found by simulating this missing piece with a lock washer, it solves all problems with the hydraulics and the bleeding of the system. In conclusion, Never having any shifting problems again. Since I performed this fix, I haven't had any problems with shifting at all. It's nice and smooth. I've had it this way for almost 2 years now, and I know there is no extra strain on any part of the clutch including the throwout bearing. I really think the GM not only messed up on manufacturing the push-rod, but also created a weak clutch pedal bracket, because it also appears the my clutch pedal assembly is kinda warped. I don't think that this was important enough for any kind of GM recall. Besides they had engine fires to worry about. I'm still waiting for my first fire, LOL   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. In case you don't have the Haynes manual? click this to see diagram 4.5 <A HREF="http://eccentrix.com/business/cityselect/haynesdiagram.html" TARGET=_blank>http://eccentrix.com/business/cityselect/haynesdiagram.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Fiero Fanatic (edited 01-14-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000033||Fiero Fanatic||01-14-2001||06:41 AM||||FACT: For as quick and simple as this is to do, It's worth a try for an eternity of smooth shifting. After all, it's 2 years and counting for me   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Except for my broken timing gear   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> But that's a whole different story.<p>[This message has been edited by Fiero Fanatic (edited 01-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||DJRice||01-15-2001||10:19 AM||||Fanatic, <P>If you have an 84 then you have an aluminum clutch pedal and it is highly likely that your clutch pedal assembly is bent/warped.  Get a clutch from an 87/88 to solve that problem.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000035||cowans||01-15-2001||03:32 PM||||Fiero Fanatic, your photos do not show up online with either Netscape or Int Explorer. thanks, Sandy||||reg||1
Z||000036||chrissherman||01-15-2001||03:50 PM||||I was having clutch and shifting problems after rebuilding the master cyl come to find out the master rod loop points up problems solaved P.S I used two canaden dimes concaved with a carage bolt and a minne slege as a spacer betwine the rod and the plunger no more play!!!!||||reg||7
Z||000037||Fiero Fanatic||01-16-2001||01:27 AM||||I have tested and re-tested these links. Can anyone tell me what exactly their getting when they click on them? There should be absolutely no reason why they don't work. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000038||Fiero Fanatic||01-16-2001||01:42 AM||||Here is a simple clutch test to see if my fix will work for you. Pump your clutch pedal without completely depressing the clutch, so you can build hydraulic pressure. Without depressing the pedal, push the pedal all the way to the floor. Try shifting your gears. Try all drive gears then reverse. You should be able to shift just fine. Then depress your clutch pedal completely, then push your pedal to the floor again. You now shouldn't be able to shift at all or not as well. This means that your plunger is allowing air into your clutch master cylinder. The way to fix it is by installing a dime size lock washer on to your push-rod between your retaining washer and the round end of the push-rod. If you can see the pics by clicking the links, you'll see what I mean. I can see them and I have both Netscape 6 and IE 5.5   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Anyone please again let me know what your getting by clicking them. It will give me a better clue on fixing them.<p>[This message has been edited by Fiero Fanatic (edited 01-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000039||DJRice||01-16-2001||09:23 AM||||I just took a look at the source code for the page that is supposed to load that picture and found your problem.  <P>You have this:<BR><B><I>"file:///C:/My%20Documents/fiero_info.gif"</B></I><P>That is the address to load the picture off of your local hard drive.  Since you have it on your local drive you are the only one that can see it.  If you copied that pic to web hosting drive then all you have to do is change the code in your page to this:<P><B><I>"http://www.eccentrix.com/business/cityselect/fiero_info.gif" width="388"<BR>height="324"</B></I><P>Hope this helps.  If you dont understand what any of this means, then you need to download PIP (follow link at the bottom of the page) and copy it to the forum server.  <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
