Z||000043||Tryxalon||01-20-2001||11:14 PM||||Well, this looks like something totally different than the problem I had on my '85 SE 5sp Duke. <P>After replacing the slave, the clutch pedal and bushings, the master cylinder and the line I still could not get sufficient throw to disengage the clutch. I could get 11/16ths but not the 15/16ths needed to actually disengage the clutch. <P>Frustration finally kicking me into analysis mode I did some very careful and accurate measurements I found out that there was a difference of .6 cubic inches in the actuating volume of the Master Cylinder and that of the Slave cylinder. Bores and lengths of actuation were totally different.  <P>Of course, physics says a smaller volume cannot move a larger volume an equal distance. As others have noted, a longer pushrod would correct the volume difference but only by changing the engagement angles and cause excessive wear and tear on both the slave cylinder and the throwout bearing. <P> The only logical result was to modify the slave cylinder to have equal or less volume than the master. I did this by cutting off a portion of a brass piston from the old slave cylinder with it's o-ring, and carefully matching it to the piston in the slave, "adjusting the volume". Not totally my idea, but "bodged" or "mcguyvered" based on an idea from a poster on one of these boards. <P>When properly bled, the overall system volume is sufficiently reduced and leaves sufficient travel and throw to disengage the brand new clutch (w/ TO). As the clutch wears in, it has accepted additional fluid to adjust itself. The geometry of the master and the slave actuation is not changed. <P>||||reg||1
